.TH DLIST_MOVE_TAIL 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
dlist_move_tail \- Move the tail of a doubly linked list to another list

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/dlist.h>

.IB void
.IB dlist_move_tail(dlist* from, dlist* to);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBdlist_move_tail\fR function moves the tail of a doubly linked list \fBfrom\fR to the end of another list \fBto\fR. If \fBto\fR is initially empty, it simply becomes the same as \fBfrom\fR. If \fBto\fR is not empty, the tail of \fBfrom\fR is appended to the tail of \fBto\fR, and \fBfrom\fR becomes empty.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB from
The doubly linked list from which the tail is to be moved.
.TP
.IB to
The doubly linked list to which the tail is to be moved.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function does not return a value.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBdlist_move_tail\fR function:

.nf
#include <lib/dlist.h>

int main() {
    dlist from, to;
    dlist_init(&from);
    dlist_init(&to);

    // Add some elements to from
    // ...

    dlist_move_tail(&from, &to); // Move the tail of from to to

    // from is now empty, and to contains the elements from from

    return 0;
}
.nef